<h1>1.2 Your first cache: IMemoryCache and cache-aside</h1>
<p class="lead">You will compare reading a product straight from the database with reading it through an in-memory cache, and then break the cache on purpose to see its weaknesses.</p>

<div class="presenter" data-video="1.2" hidden></div>
<div class="story" data-story="1.2"></div>

<div class="rw-head"><h2>&#127757; In the real world</h2></div>
<p class="rw-sub">How the biggest companies on the internet meet the idea from this lesson.</p>
<div class="rw-grid">
    <div class="rw-card" style="--rw:#1877f2">
        <div class="rw-company"><span class="rw-logo">📘</span>Facebook</div>
        <div class="rw-stat">Look-aside cache<small>the same pattern you just built</small></div>
        <p class="rw-text">Facebook&#x27;s memcache is a &quot;demand-filled look-aside cache&quot;: the web server checks memcache, reads the database on a miss, and puts the value in memcache. That is exactly the cache-aside code in this lesson, running across thousands of servers.</p>
        <div class="rw-lesson"><b>Lesson:</b> Cache-aside is not a toy pattern: it powers the biggest sites.</div>
        <div class="rw-source">Source: &quot;Scaling Memcache at Facebook&quot;, NSDI 2013</div>
    </div>
    <div class="rw-card" style="--rw:#1877f2">
        <div class="rw-company"><span class="rw-logo">📘</span>Facebook</div>
        <div class="rw-stat">Delete, don&#x27;t update<small>how they handle writes</small></div>
        <p class="rw-text">When data changes, Facebook deletes the cache key instead of writing the new value into it. Deletes are idempotent: doing them twice is harmless. This is the &quot;Remove from cache&quot; fix you used for stale data.</p>
        <div class="rw-lesson"><b>Lesson:</b> Removing a key after a change is the simplest safe invalidation.</div>
        <div class="rw-source">Source: &quot;Scaling Memcache at Facebook&quot;, NSDI 2013</div>
    </div>
</div>



<details class="deep">
<summary>&#128214; Want to understand it deeply? Read the full explanation</summary>

<h2>What is IMemoryCache?</h2>
<div class="story mini" data-story="1.2-reference"></div>
<p><code>IMemoryCache</code> is the cache built into .NET. It is a thread-safe dictionary that lives <b>inside your application's memory</b> and supports expiration and size limits. You turn it on with <code>services.AddMemoryCache()</code> and inject it wherever you need it.</p>
<div class="grid grid-2">
    <div class="card">
        <b>Strengths</b>
        <ul>
            <li>Extremely fast: it stores the object itself, no network, no serialization</li>
            <li>Zero extra infrastructure</li>
            <li>Perfect for small, read-heavy data on a single server</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
        <b>Limits</b>
        <ul>
            <li>Lost when the app restarts (the cache becomes cold)</li>
            <li>Each server has its own copy. With 3 servers you have 3 different caches that can disagree. Level 4 solves this with Redis</li>
            <li>Uses your app's RAM, so it needs limits (lesson 1.4)</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<h2>The pattern: cache-aside (lazy loading)</h2>
<p>The application itself checks the cache first and only goes to the database on a miss. The cache sits "aside" and knows nothing about the database. It is the most common caching pattern, and you will compare it with the others in Level 3.</p>
<div class="flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>1. Build the key</b><small><code>level1:product:1</code></small></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>2. Look in the cache</b><small><code>TryGetValue</code></small></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</div>
    <div class="flow-step hit"><b>Hit</b><small>Return the cached value. Done.</small></div>
</div>
<div class="flow">
    <div class="flow-step miss"><b>Miss</b><small>Not found</small></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>3. Load from database</b><small>Slow (800 ms here)</small></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>4. Store in the cache</b><small><code>Set(key, value, 60s)</code></small></div>
    <div class="flow-arrow">&rarr;</div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>5. Return</b><small>The next request is a hit</small></div>
</div>
<p>The heart of <code>MemoryCacheBasicService.GetWithCacheAsync</code>, slightly shortened:</p>
<pre><code>if (cache.TryGetValue(key, out CachedValue&lt;ProductDto&gt;? cached) &amp;&amp; cached is not null)
{
    return new CacheResultDto&lt;ProductDto&gt; { Data = cached.Value, Source = "Cache" };
}

var product = await repository.GetByIdAsync(id);
var value = new CachedValue&lt;ProductDto&gt;(product.ToDto(), Duration, null);
cache.Set(key, value, Duration);
return new CacheResultDto&lt;ProductDto&gt; { Data = value.Value, Source = "Database" };</code></pre>
<p class="muted"><code>CachedValue&lt;T&gt;</code> is a small wrapper that remembers when the value was cached and when it expires, only so this portal can show it to you. In normal code you would usually cache the DTO directly.</p>

</details>

