<h2>Experiments: do these in order</h2>
<ol class="steps">
    <li><b>Feel the database.</b> Click <em>Get without cache</em> three times. Every request takes about 800 ms because every request goes to the database.</li>
    <li><b>First miss.</b> Click <em>Get with cache</em>. The first time is a <span class="badge miss">Miss</span>: it is just as slow as the database, plus a tiny bit to store the value. The cache does not make the first request faster.</li>
    <li><b>Hits.</b> Click <em>Send 5 cached requests</em>. All five are <span class="badge hit">Hit</span> and take a few thousandths of a millisecond. Look at the hit ratio climbing.</li>
    <li><b>Stale data.</b> Click <em>Change price in database</em>. It updates the price through the normal product API. Now click <em>Get with cache</em>: you still get the <b>old</b> price, because the cache does not know the database changed. Click <em>Get without cache</em> to see the real price.</li>
    <li><b>Fix it by removing.</b> Click <em>Remove from cache</em>, then <em>Get with cache</em>. It is a miss again and now returns the new price. Removing a key after a change is the simplest form of invalidation.</li>
    <li><b>Things that do not exist.</b> Select product 999 and click <em>Get with cache</em> several times. Every request is a slow miss, because nothing gets cached when the product is not found. This is called <em>cache penetration</em>, and you will fix it in Level 7.</li>
    <li><b>Restart.</b> Stop and start the app, then click <em>Get with cache</em>. The cache is cold again. In-memory caches do not survive restarts.</li>
</ol>

<h2>What you should take away</h2>
<div class="callout tip">
    <strong>Key points</strong>
    A hit is thousands of times faster than a miss. The first request is always a miss. A cache is a copy, so after the source changes the copy is stale until it expires or is removed. In-memory means per process, so it is gone after a restart and not shared between servers.
</div>
<div class="callout danger">
    <strong>Common mistake: changing a cached object</strong>
    IMemoryCache stores a <b>reference</b> to your object, not a copy. If code does <code>var p = cache.Get&lt;Product&gt;(key); p.Price = 0;</code> then every later reader gets price 0. Cache DTOs that nobody modifies (or immutable records) and never cache EF Core tracked entities.
</div>

